01 plan-writer done [9.9m 84 turns $3.87 · opus · default · 1 refused]: The plan (600 lines) is written and self-reviewed. It adds a "⋯" button to every message that allows an action, at every width, and removes the hover-only row. The menu draws itself above the page, so the scrolling list or thread panel can't clip it. It works by keyboard, only one is open at a time, and a pure function decides what each message offers. The test approach is TDD at the Vitest layer plus the one BDD journey the issue asks for: Ada on a 1024×768 touch tablet reacts and replies through "⋯", Grace sees both live, and the existing edit, delete, react and reply journeys go through "⋯" via the page objects. Five decisions are assumed: where focus goes when "⋯" disappears (thread panel case), a menu closing when its actions change, an outside tap not moving focus, the menu following a scroll instead of closing, and "⋯" staying while editing. The last message of a long channel and the thread panel's right edge are left to the acceptance pack.
